/target/

# The oracle. `reference/*.stats.json` is dumped by the Python analyser from a
# real export, and it carries chat content verbatim -- `superlatives` quotes
# message text, `people` and `graph` carry everyone's names. Committing it
# publishes other people's conversation, and git keeps it for good.
#
# Same rule as telegram_rust's /reference/, and for the same reason: without
# it the corpus tests skip, and that skip has to be loud.
/reference/

# Today's output, recorded before the refactor started and compared against
# after every step of it. Same content as /reference/ and the same rule: these
# are whole reports on two real archives, so they are somebody else's
# conversation and they do not go into git. `save.bat baseline` records them;
# `save.bat baseline check` compares.
/baseline/

# Reports written while testing. Each is a self-contained megabyte of somebody
# else's archive.
report*.html
*.stats.json

# ...except the golden's input, which is the one stats dump that is safe to
# commit: `crates/tga-report/tests/fixture.stats.json` is hand-written and
# names nobody. Without this line the rule above swallows it and the golden
# test fails on a fresh clone with "no fixture" rather than with a diff.
!crates/tga-report/tests/fixture.stats.json

# The built binaries. The folder itself is tracked (dist/.gitkeep) so a fresh
# clone has somewhere to build into; its contents never are -- a 15 MB exe
# rebuilt on every release is not something to keep the history of.
#
# Note the shape: `/dist/` alone would exclude the directory outright and git
# would then never look inside it, so the negation below could not re-include
# anything. `/dist/*` excludes the contents and leaves the directory itself
# visible, which is what makes `!` work at all.
/dist/*
!/dist/.gitkeep

# Local scratch
*.log
